#basePath P1_Lamprey
#include "Header.leaf"

:: Start
@exposed
@trigger    OnLocationChanged
// "NONE" ensures no other threads are in-progress
// global:currentPhase == 1 ensures player is in phase 1
@when       global:currentThread == "NONE", global:currentPhase == 1, location == "ResearchLab"
@cutscene
// Lock all location on map
$call SetInThreadLocks()
$call DisableMapButton()
$call SetBackground("bg/bg-research-lab.png")

// Mark start of thread
$set global:currentThread = "P1_Lamprey"

$call  ClearVisibleLines()
{@}   
       The university. What better place to learn about sea lamprey than at the very place researching them?
       You knock on the door to the lab. 
       After a moment, a young man in a fashionably tailored lab coat opens it.

{@Arjan}
       Hello? Oh, yeah. I've been waiting for you. 
       Like a psychic. 
       Haha.
       No, Saoirse called me to say you'd stop by. Come in. 

{@}
       You follow him. 
       A phonograph plays old-timey jazz, jumping every few beats like rocks skidding on a lake. 

{@Arjan}
       Oh. Sorry about that. It was, like, 75% off. 
       So, what can I interest you in? Chemicals? Coffee? Student loans?

{@You}
       I think I'll pass on those, but I do need your help. 

{@}
       You hand him the photo of the bite on the fish. //photo pops up again?

{@Arjan}
       Sea lamprey. Huh.
       They're an interesting specimen. Some might call them... 
       Fishy. Yeah, that's a good one. 

{@You}
       Look, I don't know a lot about sea lamprey. 
       Any information you can share with me will be really helpful.

{@Arjan}
       I can definitely help you with that. 

{@}
       Arjan walks over to his desk and rummages around for a moment before pulling out a thick, dust-covered book. 
       He pulls a photo from between its pages and hands it to you. //photo added to field notes

{@Arjan}
       Sea lamprey are a kind of fish, but unlike most fish, they don't have bones or a jaw. 
       Their mouth is a sucking disk filled with teeth and a rasping tongue.
       Sea lampreys are parasites, which means that they get their nutrients from another host organism.
       In this case, the sea lamprey stick onto other fish through their teeth and then feed off the fish's bodily fluids like a leech. 

{@You}
       Eugh. That's kinda gross. 

{@Arjan}
       They're definitely different from us, and they can look pretty scary, but parasites are an important part of the food chain.
       The issue with sea lamprey is that in the Great Lakes, which is outside of their indigenous range, their prey don't have any protection against them. 
       So the sea lamprey can really hurt the populations of fish like ciscoes and lake trout in the Great Lakes. // evidence chip here?

{@You}
       Which is what I've been tasked to do: to balance the population of sea lamprey with the other organisms living in the Great Lakes. 
       Hm. Thanks for the information, Arjan. I appreciate it. 

{@Arjan}
       Of course. Come back if you want to talk more about new technologies that are being developed to manage sea lamprey populations. 

{@}
       You smile and head out of the lab. 
       Taking the photo of the sea lamprey out of your pocket, you examine its strange, round smile. 
       How come you return home and feel out of place, but it comes from far away and wins?
       
$call GiveEvidenceChip("Evidence-Lamprey-Discovered")

{@}
       [Open your map to continue]

// skip to end
$goto .End


:: End
@exposed
@tracked
// Unlock next locations
$call UnlockLocation(DNROffice)  // P1_NativeLamprey

// Mark end of thread
$set global:currentThread = "NONE"

// Go to Phase 1 Continue Hub
$goto P1_Hub.Continue